      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/10d3d9a3b026fc1511d9e7a57c1f3276606c9397f0045fdb7748e3038a42fb21.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $P(x)⇒Q(x)≡¬P(x)∨Q(x),$ the corresponding truth sets can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}:$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/f02e2138f843c5a350c8be6c81a408c4af51fb9b19ced3fa26bfc466493008ac.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, to get the truth set of $P(x)∨Q(x),$ we take all the points that belong to ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{p}$ or ${T}_{Q}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/45e38853893ceec19f969a1611cd50bbb9ff97e7bfd617c6e2e0a6268fb4b8fa.png)\n\nAs a result, we get the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/dde2aacce3d0707ddb09c06ca305645eadba1aedca9858eacc24abd46d09b03c.png)",
